    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y4gfsen",
      "content": "Electricity is a form of energy produced due to the flow of electric charges. In daily life, electricity is used in:"
    },
    {
      "id": "04wb6dx",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Lighting</li><li>Fans</li><li>Heaters</li><li>Mobile phones</li><li>Computers</li></ul>",
        "Lighting",
        "Fans",
        "Heaters",
        "Mobile phones",
        "Computers"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xb98xdq",
      "content": "Electricity plays a very important role in modern life."
    },
    {
      "id": "ubtp8dv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC CHARGE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "owlavqm",
      "content": "Electric charge is the basic property of matter responsible for electrical phenomena. Types of charges:"
    },
    {
      "id": "kho3nye",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li><img></img>Positive charge</li><li>Negative charge</li></ul>",
        "<img></img>Positive charge",
        "Negative charge"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "49h4sey",
      "content": "Like charges repel each other. Unlike charges attract each other."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "t4ev58u",
      "content": "SI unit of charge = Coulomb (C)"
    },
    {
      "id": "7d1azn0",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC CURRENT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "x7v9gba",
      "content": "Electric current is the rate of flow of electric charge. Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "nt3kj8v",
      "content": "Current (I) = Charge (Q) / Time (t) SI unit of current = Ampere (A)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a9wrpuk",
      "content": "1 Ampere means 1 Coulomb of charge flowing per second."
    },
    {
      "id": "lmxu3d5",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC CIRCUIT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fo0f6jf",
      "content": "An electric circuit is a closed path through which electric current flows. Main components:"
    },
    {
      "id": "z2r6tfm",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Cell or battery</li><li>Connecting wires</li><li>Switch</li><li>Load (bulb, fan, etc.)</li></ul>",
        "Cell or battery",
        "Connecting wires",
        "Switch",
        "Load (bulb, fan, etc.)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "nwr8ft3",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC POTENTIAL DIFFERENCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kscn1bh",
      "content": "Potential difference is the work done in moving a unit charge from one point to another. Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s472r6i",
      "content": "V = Work / Charge SI unit = Volt (V)"
    },
    {
      "id": "w5ltfyu",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "OHM’S LAW"
    },
    {
      "id": "u7unxip",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "ohuovuo",
      "src": "/books/Precis/SSC COMPLETE GK/images/image_ohuovuo.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "2tbwczs",
      "content": "<img></img>According to Ohm’s Law:"
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Ohm’s Law states that the current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across its ends, provided physical conditions such as temperature remain constant.",
      "id": "2g1877n"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "v4jtz66",
      "content": "The current flowing through a conductor is directly proportional to the potential difference across its ends, provided temperature remains constant."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "bq813gb",
      "content": "Formula: "
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "hhnj66v",
      "content": "V = IR"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "610f0lh",
      "content": "Where:"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "540jvbr",
      "content": "V = Voltage I = Current"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "q9bnchv",
      "content": "R = Resistance"
    },
    {
      "id": "jlajrhv",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "RESISTANCE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gxvg4kj",
      "content": "Resistance is the opposition offered to the flow of electric current. SI unit = Ohm (Ω)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "myufoph",
      "content": "Factors affecting resistance:"
    },
    {
      "id": "wlrps3d",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Length of conductor</li><li>Area of cross-section</li><li>Material of conductor</li><li>Temperature</li></ul>",
        "Length of conductor",
        "Area of cross-section",
        "Material of conductor",
        "Temperature"
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "xz4o83n",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC POWER"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "b7l2lor",
      "content": "Electric power is the rate at which electrical energy is consumed. Formula:"
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Parameter",
        "Series Combination",
        "Parallel Combination"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Electric Current (I)",
          "Remains same through all resistors",
          "Divides among individual branches"
        ],
        [
          "Potential Difference (V)",
          "Divides across each resistor",
          "Remains same across all resistors"
        ],
        [
          "Equivalent Resistance",
          "R_eq = R1 + R2 + R3 (Maximum)",
          "1/R_eq = 1/R1 + 1/R2 + 1/R3 (Minimum)"
        ],
        [
          "Impact of Fault",
          "Circuit breaks; all devices stop working",
          "Other devices continue to operate independently"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "dgi0vlt"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gjv1qxj",
      "content": "Power (P) = V × I SI unit = Watt (W)"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f7amv4d",
      "content": "Other forms:"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "rvvbk62",
      "content": "P = I²R"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "2ld6tiv",
      "content": "P = V² / R"
    },
    {
      "id": "lliarll",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC ENERGY"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "l93plfl",
      "content": "Electric energy is the total electrical work done. Commercial unit of electric energy = kilowatt-hour (kWh) 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xt3auu6",
      "content": "This is also called one unit of electricity."
    },
    {
      "id": "ipigo3u",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "HEATING EFFECT OF ELECTRIC CURRENT"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "trfk2mq",
      "content": "When electric current flows through a conductor, it produces heat. This effect is used in:"
    },
    {
      "id": "yecn9cw",
      "type": "list",
      "items": [
        "<ul><li>Electric heater</li><li>Iron</li><li>Geyser</li><li>Electric bulb</li></ul>",
