    {
      "id": "dj7d16m",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "ELECTRIC FUSE"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0zooljd",
      "content": "Fuse is a safety device used to protect electrical appliances. It melts when excess current flows and breaks the circuit."
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "An Ammeter is always connected in series due to low resistance, whereas a Voltmeter is connected in parallel due to high resistance.",
        "Electric fuse wires are designed with high resistance and a low melting point (typically an alloy of tin and lead).",
        "Commercial unit of electric energy is Kilowatt-hour (kWh), where 1 unit = 1 kWh = 3.6 × 10⁶ Joules.",
        "In series combination, the current remains constant across all resistors, while in parallel combination, the potential difference remains the same."
      ],
      "id": "vwtge7l"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
